      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Both frameworks offer strong moral foundations, but they respond to justice from different ethical priorities: retributivism focuses on moral desert and proportional punishment, while restorative justice emphasizes repairing harm and societal healing. The \"better\" choice depends heavily on whether your course materials prioritize accountability for past wrongs or the moral imperative to restore community trust and prevent future harm. Ultimately, the most nuanced ethical view often argues that restorative approaches better align with the core legal value of justice as fairness by addressing the systemic needs of all parties involved.\n\n**Final Answer: There is no single \"more\" morally sound framework without context, as they address different primary ethical concerns of justice.**\n</think>\n\nBoth frameworks offer strong moral foundations, but they respond to justice from different ethical priorities: retributivism focuses on moral desert and proportional punishment, while restorative justice emphasizes repairing harm and societal healing. The \"better\" choice depends heavily on whether your course materials prioritize accountability for past wrongs or the moral imperative to restore community trust and prevent future harm. Ultimately, the most nuanced ethical view often argues that restorative approaches better align with the core legal value of \"justice as fairness\" by addressing the systemic needs of all parties involved.",
